d74e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be9aa3b1-68a0-5981-aa70-f10a99f6fbac" :question/id #uuid "be9aa3b1-68a0-5981-aa70-f10a99f6fbac" :question/math-academy-id "q-253556" :question/problem "Consider the set $C=(-2,-1].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(C).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $C$ is the {{field-1}} of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $C$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $C.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $C.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $C.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $C$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $C,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(C)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the maximum of $C.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $C$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(C)=-1\\in C.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cccdc7ac-4c98-5946-bd46-0e11422f5ea2" "answer-field-aa59b90b-b072-5672-8d8f-940ec810e0d0" "answer-field-4b4c07c6-ea87-547e-81bb-3747ed48d208" "answer-field-cac80e9a-f2fc-5d5a-bce9-bb59b0d3e2fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cccdc7ac-4c98-5946-bd46-0e11422f5ea2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cccdc7ac-4c98-5946-bd46-0e11422f5ea2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-fi