nf(A)=max(-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $0$ is also the minimum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ bound of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(A)=0\\in A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0bfad37a-ed5d-5ba6-af95-c7259e94ef98" "answer-field-fadb4645-7f64-5b96-9f43-b05796106137" "answer-field-da62234a-a027-5829-82b2-366ee9d80a0b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160496 :answer-field/correct 17592186160499 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0bfad37a-ed5d-5ba6-af95-c7259e94ef98" :answer-field/id #uuid "0bfad37a-ed5d-5ba6-af95-c7259e94ef98" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6d4dd4d1-b3f1-5589-a143-db8eb6adb8e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1881584d-67a8-5364-9525-db8912138dbc" "answer-4a30385b-0a6e-5893-a6d2-8df76ccd6427" "answer-e3a79cf3-8c17-52fa-90f1-a48643bb37ab" "answer-6d4dd4d1-b3f1-5589-a143-db8eb6adb8e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1881584d-67a8-5364-9525-db8912138dbc" :answer/id #uuid "1881584d-67a8-5364-9525-db8912138dbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a30385b-0a6e-5893-a6d2-8df76ccd6427" :answer/id #uuid "4a30385b-0a6e-5893-a6d2-8df76ccd6427" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value ">" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3a79cf3-8c17-52fa-90f1-a48643bb37ab" :answer/id #uuid "e3a79cf3-8c17-52fa-90f1-a48643bb37ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ge " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d4dd4d1-b3f1-5589-a143-db8eb6adb8e1" :answer/id #uuid "6d4dd4d1-b3f1-5589-a143-db8eb6adb8e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fadb4645-7f64-5b96-9f43-b05796106137" :answer-field/id #uuid "fadb4645-7f64-5b96-9f43-b05796106137" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-183c7bd1-c965-51f8-b1ac-7a7863aba7de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-183c7bd1-c965-51f8-b1ac-7a7863aba7de" "answer-1930fe5c-7248-5f8e-9be2-f6fdc0db788e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-183c7bd1-c965-51f8-b1ac-7a7863aba7de" :answer/id #uuid "183c7bd1-c965-51f8-b1ac-7a7863aba7de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1930fe5c-7248-5f8e-9be2-f6fdc0db788e" :answer/id #uuid "1930fe5c-7248-5f8e-9be2-f6fdc0db788e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da62234a-a027-5829-82b2-366ee9d80a0b" :answer-field/id #uuid "da62234a-a027-5829-82b2-366ee9d80a0b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d46f4c55-e885-52a7-91a7-0ed32488df0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7096badc-84ea-5b18-b70e-39adbbb84813" "answer-d46f4c55-e885-52a7-91a7-0ed32488df0c" "answer-aedae25b-d802-5520-8495-d5e56176a14b" "answer-8e1362b2-4193-58f2-bc51-d5db16366ce1" "answer-ee425291-e6b5-5c07-9269-4a392656def2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7096badc-84ea-5b18-b70e-39adbbb84813" :answer/id #uuid "7096badc-84ea-5b18-b70e-39adbbb84813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d46f4c55-e885-52a7-91a7-0ed32488df0c" :answer/id #uuid "d46f4c55-e885-52a7-91a7-0ed32488df0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aedae25b-d802-5520-8495-d5e56176a14b" :answer/id #uuid "aedae25b-d802-5520-8495-d5e56176a14b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e1362b2-4193-58f2-bc51-d5db16366ce1" :answer/id #uuid "8e1362b2-4193-58f2-bc51-d5db16366ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee425291-e6b5-5c07-9269-4a392656def2" :answer/id #uuid "ee425291-e6b5-5c07-9269-4a392656def2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" :question/id #uuid "fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253912" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/8b/8be6d8824c8cbf1fb66d64d3ccc027f2949149adc9f95cf4d1f3a814baec80b3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $-1.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-1\\notin A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73153ef3-6520-50a9-82e3-ddb42b9894a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73153ef3-6520-50a9-82e3-ddb42b9894a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "73153ef3-6520-50a9-82e3-ddb42b9894a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65aa0c48-3279-5f91-ae4c-1449b2527192" "answer-d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" "answer-de845076-a6ce-55d2-9cd1-cde3a48b2d44" "answer-c1ad51fd-d8f5-59d7-8e16-ad0e1d154496" "answer-0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65aa0c48-3279-5f91-ae4c-1449b2527192" :answer/id #uuid "65aa0c48-3279-5f91-ae4c-1449b2527192" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/id #uuid "d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de845076-a6ce-55d2-9cd1-cde3a48b2d44" :answer/id #uuid "de845076-a6ce-55d2-9cd1-cde3a48b2d44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1ad51fd-d8f5-59d7-8e16-ad0e1d154496" :answer/id #uuid "c1ad51fd-d8f5-59d7-8e16-ad0e1d154496" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b" :answer/id #uuid "0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" :question/id #uuid "aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" :question/math-academy-id "q-253910" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4df8d23-1851-528c-ba22-425776fee81a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4df8d23-1851-528c-ba22-425776fee81a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4df8d23-1851-528c-ba22-425776fee81a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" "answer-39e361d5-1d4c-5a24-9857-936433e94187" "answer-314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" "answer-c27fc419-17a3-5364-b645-76f73a7ab6e3" "answer-3a52c1c9-d934-5a2b-a0a5-58f417b57457"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/id #uuid "0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39e361d5-1d4c-5a24-9857-936433e94187" :answer/id #uuid "39e361d5-1d4c-5a24-9857-936433e94187" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" :answer/id #uuid "314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c27fc419-17a3-5364-b645-76f73a7ab6e3" :answer/id #uuid "c27fc419-17a3-5364-b645-76f73a7ab6e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a52c1c9-d934-5a2b-a0a5-58f417b57457" :answer/id #uuid "3a52c1c9-d934-5a2b-a0a5-58f417b57457" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13b42a92-bc61-5733-b661-85ce69e84b97" :question/id #uuid "13b42a92-bc61-5733-b661-85ce69e84b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-253513" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/35/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d4e4ded-e058-51a6-b1cd-e47d3c47538e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d4e4ded-e058-51a6-b1cd-e47d3c47538e" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d4e4ded-e058-51a6-b1cd-e47d3c47538e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c019112a-3b1b-5e5d-bb33-cb7e429db013" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f73ae8d-f2e8-5cf5-bf54-d34ec79a90c7" "answer-42fc1632-c9c1-5009-88f7-185a50c722d7" "answer-c019112a-3b1b-5e5d-bb33-cb7e429db013" "answer-f49f1a1a-8ba9-5ac6-a28d-df18540cdfb9" "answer-34e09dab-11a6-5462-9d88-bddc2bd9213b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f73ae8d-f2e8-5cf5-bf54-d34ec79a90c7" :answer/id #uuid "2f73ae8d-f2e8-5cf5-bf54-d34ec79a90c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=3\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42fc1632-c9c1-5009-88f7-185a50c722d7" :answer/id #uuid "42fc1632-c9c1-5009-88f7-185a50c722d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=3\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c019112a-3b1b-5e5d-bb33-cb7e429db013" :answer/id #uuid "c019112a-3b1b-5e5d-bb33-cb7e429db013" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f49f1a1a-8ba9-5ac6-a28d-df18540cdfb9" :answer/id #uuid "f49f1a1a-8ba9-5ac6-a28d-df18540cdfb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34e09dab-11a6-5462-9d88-bddc2bd9213b" :answer/id #uuid "34e09dab-11a6-5462-9d88-bddc2bd9213b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" :question/id #uuid "79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-253831" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6d681e8-6c35-50f8-ba73-9aaaae945560"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6d681e8-6c35-50f8-ba73-9aaaae945560" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d681e8-6c35-50f8-ba73-9aaaae945560" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" "answer-b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" "answer-970f9583-5c69-59ca-ba57-45f24412729b" "answer-96d1ac88-15ae-5c73-a141-867400b768e6" "answer-bb516a43-df07-5641-b235-fe98c46c7066"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" :answer/id #uuid "21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer/id #uuid "b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-970f9583-5c69-59ca-ba57-45f24412729b" :answer/id #uuid "970f9583-5c69-59ca-ba57-45f24412729b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96d1ac88-15ae-5c73-a141-867400b768e6" :answer/id #uuid "96d1ac88-15ae-5c73-a141-867400b768e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb516a43-df07-5641-b235-fe98c46c7066" :answer/id #uuid "bb516a43-df07-5641-b235-fe98c46c7066" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a4eaf9cf-b8c1-5f93-bf45-1dc2cc8b5b67" :question/id #uuid "a4eaf9cf-b8c1-5f93-bf45-1dc2cc8b5b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-253525" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec0ca0f3-190b-55df-a54b-77392ca10b39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec0ca0f3-190b-55df-a54b-77392ca10b39" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0ca0f3-190b-55df-a54b-77392ca10b39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-800778ee-d630-5816-9df2-7a05075e38f0" "answer-95715de7-9216-56ef-b8bf-3a456d9858be" "answer-31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" "answer-1fa61d86-b0f9-5a61-a276-87b1e8131516" "answer-7e78293d-aec3-58b5-a329-6318d6d9600d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-800778ee-d630-5816-9df2-7a05075e38f0" :answer/id #uuid "800778ee-d630-5816-9df2-7a05075e38f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95715de7-9216-56ef-b8bf-3a456d9858be" :answer/id #uuid "95715de7-9216-56ef-b8bf-3a456d9858be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" :answer/id #uuid "31002c35-5985-5fde-b87e-39583a093fde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa61d86-b0f9-5a61-a276-87b1e8131516" :answer/id #uuid "1fa61d86-b0f9-5a61-a276-87b1e8131516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e78293d-aec3-58b5-a329-6318d6d9600d" :answer/id #uuid "7e78293d-aec3-58b5-a329-6318d6d9600d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162532 :question/problem "![](images/db/db233bb76aeb4692d932967a8a61dd7ad675a7df7adb1a006568cf12d25d5511.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160509 :answer-field/correct "answer-a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" "answer-977220d8-a69f-5fe3-9d16-eebe5663a8a5" "answer-81c15513-de6c-507a-a4e4-85a00ef2493e" "answer-8e411409-7d1f-5737-886f-eec8dd70737b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" :answer/id #uuid "a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-977220d8-a69f-5fe3-9d16-eebe5663a8a5" :answer/id #uuid "977220d8-a69f-5fe3-9d16-eebe5663a8a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c15513-de6c-507a-a4e4-85a00ef2493e" :answer/id #uuid "81c15513-de6c-507a-a4e4-85a00ef2493e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e411409-7d1f-5737-886f-eec8dd70737b" :answer/id #uuid "8e411409-7d1f-5737-886f-eec8dd70737b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" :question/id #uuid "956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253859" :question/problem "![](images/37/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e79c778e-75ba-5f9f-8b03-a34683fe89e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e79c778e-75ba-5f9f-8b03-a34683fe89e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "e79c778e-75ba-5f9f-8b03-a34683fe89e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer/id #uuid "903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a350bae-823f-50ee-90ca-2add36d5d3d7" :question/id #uuid "1a350bae-823f-50ee-90ca-2add36d5d3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-253896" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d745d274250c84f6701ff22d17d0c6506a9f03f4a1a461e09f609a0ea11d99b.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c08a640-f274-5c79-a1a1-0eb159327882"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c08a640-f274-5c79-a1a1-0eb159327882" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c08a640-f274-5c79-a1a1-0eb159327882" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e" :answer/id #uuid "e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b171f8f-d26a-5943-99d9-8e4c4110d0e4" :question/id #uuid "5b171f8f-d26a-5943-99d9-8e4c4110d0e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253899" :question/problem "![](images/56/567aa249f2ed84b537d20d4137a28e0df1cd3e4c942f4cf305bd864da32b8bac.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22e3bb45-2a66-5b11-9cff-86ec4e98fa2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22e3bb45-2a66-5b11-9cff-86ec4e98fa2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "22e3bb45-2a66-5b11-9cff-86ec4e98fa2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" "answer-5df91ad0-b4ef-5be9-8cb0-c61b9391c511" "answer-999b27c8-912c-51fc-a11a-66b74ddf77ac" "answer-560fbf54-d06b-5360-a195-76877c86b33e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" :answer/id #uuid "d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5df91ad0-b4ef-5be9-8cb0-c61b9391c511" :answer/id #uuid "5df91ad0-b4ef-5be9-8cb0-c61b9391c511" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-999b27c8-912c-51fc-a11a-66b74ddf77ac" :answer/id #uuid "999b27c8-912c-51fc-a11a-66b74ddf77ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-560fbf54-d06b-5360-a195-76877c86b33e" :answer/id #uuid "560fbf54-d06b-5360-a195-76877c86b33e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163369 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn about the **supremum** of a set.\n\nTo get started, we first need to understand a related concept: the **upper bounds** of a set.\n\nGiven a set $A,$ a number $x$ is called an upper bound for $A$ if every element of $A$ is smaller than or equal to $x.$ \nAlternatively, $x$ is greater than or equal to every element of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider $A = \\{1,2,3\\}\\subseteq \\mathbb R.$ In this case, any number $x\\geq 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$ since it is greater than or equal to $1, 2,$ and $3.$\nSo, $[3, \\infty)$ is the set of upper bounds of $A.$\n\nWe will denote the set of the upper bounds of $A$ as $U_A.$\n\nWe can now proceed with the definition of **supremum**.\n\nFor a set $A$ that is bounded above, the supremum of $A$ is the minimum of $U_A.$ It is also called the **least upper bound** of $A.$\n\nIn our example $A = \\{1,2,3\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\min(U_A) = \\min [3, \\infty) = 3.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\sup(A) = 3.$\n\nNotice that, when a set has a maximum, the supremum equals the maximum. This holds, in particular, for non-empty finite sets.\n\nThis leads to the following result:\n\nFor any non-empty finite set, $\\max(A) = \\sup(A)$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163203 :tutorial/content "Just as we defined the supremum, we now introduce the **infimum** of a set.\n\nAs we did for the supremum, we first need to define the lower bounds of a set.\n\nGiven a set $A,$ a number $x$ is called a **lower bound** for $A$ if every element of $A$ is greater than or equal to $x,$ or, alternatively, if $x$ is less than or equal to every element of $A.$\n\nFor example, for $A=\\{1,2,3\\}\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ any number $x \\leq 1$ is less than $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$ Therefore, $(-\\infty, 1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A.$\n\nWe will denote the set of the lower bounds of $A$ as $L_A.$\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the maximum of $L_A,$ or the greatest lower bound of $A.$ \n\nFor our set $A = \\{1,2,3\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\max(L_A) = \\max (-\\infty, 1] = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\inf(A) = 1.$\n\nAgain, we notice that the minimum of a set (provided that it exists) equals the infimum. As finite sets always have a minimum, this holds in particular for finite sets:\n\nFor any finite set, $\\min(A) = \\inf(A)$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163200 :tutorial/content "Let's explore the supremum and infimum of intervals, starting with a closed interval.\n\n**Example A:** $A = [0, 1].$\n\n**Observation (Min/Max):**\nRecall that if a set has a minimum and maximum, they correspond to the infimum and supremum.\nSince $0$ and $1$ are bounds contained in $A,$ we have $\\min(A)=0$ and $\\max(A)=1.$ Thus, we expect:\n\n$$\n\\inf (A) = 0, \\qquad \\sup (A) = 1.\n$$\n\n**Derivation using Sets of Bounds:**\nTo deepen our understanding, we verify this by finding the sets of lower and upper bounds ($L_A$ and $U_A$).\n\n1\\. **Infimum:** We identify $L_A$ and find its maximum.\n\n  - Any number $x \\leq 0$ is a lower bound for $A.$\n\n  - Any number $x > 0$ is *not* a lower bound. Since $0 \\in A$ and $0 < x,$ the number $x$ fails to be a lower bound.\n\n  Therefore, $L_A = (-\\infty, 0]$ and $\\inf (A) = \\max (-\\infty, 0] = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}.$\n\n2\\. **Supremum:** We identify $U_A$ and find its minimum.\n\n  - Any number $x \\geq 1$ is an upper bound for $A.$\n\n  - Any number $x < 1$ is *not* an upper bound. Since $1 \\in A$ and $1 > x,$ the number $x$ fails to be an upper bound.\n\n  Therefore, $U_A = [1, \\infty)$ and $\\sup (A) = \\min [1, \\infty) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163210 :tutorial/content "We now consider intervals where the endpoints are not necessarily included.\n\n**Example B:** $B = (0, 1).$ \nThe endpoints $0$ and $1$ are not in $B,$ so the set has **neither a maximum nor a minimum.**\n\nHowever, the logical boundaries of the set remain the same.\n\n- The set of lower bounds is $L_B = (-\\infty, 0].$\n\n$$\n\\inf (B) = \\max (-\\infty, 0] = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}.\n$$\n\n- The set of upper bounds is $U_B = [1, \\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\sup (B) = \\min [1, \\infty) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163211 :tutorial/content "We continue by considering a half-open interval.\n\n**Example C:** $C = [0, 1).$\nHere, the inclusion is mixed.\n\n- **Lower Bound:** $L_C = (-\\infty, 0].$ \n\n$$\n\\inf (C) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}\n$$\n\nSince $0 \\in C,$ it is also the **minimum.**\n\n- **Upper Bound:** $U_C = [1, \\infty).$ \n\n$$\n\\sup (C) = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\notin C,$ the set has **no maximum.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163209 :tutorial/content "So far, we have dealt with bounded sets: sets that have both upper and lower bounds. Now, we consider unbounded sets.\n\nIf a set $A$ is unbounded above, then it has no upper bounds. In this case, we write\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = +\\infty.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if a set $A$ is unbounded below, then it has no lower bounds. In this case, we write\n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the set $A = (1, \\infty).$ This set is unbounded above, so $\\sup(A) = +\\infty.$ However, $A$ is bounded below, and $\\inf(A) = 1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163201 :tutorial/content "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{ \\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n \\: : \\: n\\in \\mathbb N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_n =\\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N = \\{1,2,3,4,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/b2/b2fc98fed45efbeb0ba7a1bfefe61d2f5698ab36b6c026bf5590b63d2da0a38a.png)\n\nNotice that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence. Therefore, the first term $a_1$ is the maximum of the set. Since $A$ has a maximum, the supremum equals the maximum:\n\n$$\n\\sup(A) = \\max(A) = a_1 = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nNow, our set doesn't have a minimum: the points of the sequence get closer and closer to $0$ without reaching it. Let's find the infimum by determining the set of the lower bounds.\n\nNotice that:\n\n- any number $x \\leq 0$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x > 0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $\\left(\\dfrac 12\\right)^n$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x > 0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty, 0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\inf(A) = \\max \\big({-\\infty}, 0\\big] = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163202 :tutorial/content "The concepts of supremum and infimum can be extended to functions.\n\nGiven a function $y=f(x),$ the **supremum** and the **infimum** of $f$ are, respectively, the supremum and the infimum of the range of $f.$\n\nThe **supremum** and **infimum** of a function will be denoted as\n\n$$\n\\inf_{x\\in D} f(x), \\qquad \\text{and} \\sup_{x\\in D} f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the domain of the function.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola:\n\n![](images/69/690b179ad127181eeb316fb9ae9c8085fa6aa96c6d4729c88d176a908950e80e.png)\n\nThe range of $f$ is $A = (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nLet's find the infimum and the supremum of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. For any real number $y,$ we can find an element $x$ such that $f(x) < y.$\n\nTherefore, $\\inf_{x\\in \\mathbb R} f(x) = -\\infty.$\n\nNext, to find the supremum, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y \\geq 0$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y < 0$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $0,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[0,\\infty)$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\sup_{x\\in \\mathbb R} f(x) = \\sup(A) =\\min[0,\\infty)= 0.}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163370 :tutorial/content "**Theorem.** Suppose $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ is a non-empty set and $s$ is an upper bound of $A.$ Then $s = \\sup A$ if and only if for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that\n\n$$\ns - \\varepsilon < a.\n$$\n\n- **First, we prove that if $s = \\sup A$ then for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that $s - \\varepsilon < a.$**\n\nBy contradiction, suppose that there exists some $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that there does not exist any $a\\in A$ with $s - \\varepsilon < a.$ Then, every element of $A$ is less than or equal to $s - \\varepsilon.$ This implies that $s - \\varepsilon$ is an upper bound of $A.$\n\nSince $s$ is the least upper bound, we must have $s \\le s - \\varepsilon.$ However, since $\\varepsilon > 0,$ we know that $s - \\varepsilon < s.$ This is a contradiction.\n\n- **Next, we prove that if for all $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $a\\in A$ such that $s - \\varepsilon < a,$ then $s = \\sup A.$**\n\nAgain, by contradiction, assume $s \\neq \\sup A.$ Since $s$ is an upper bound of $A,$ the least upper bound must be strictly smaller than $s.$ Let $s_1 = \\sup A,$ so $s_1 < s.$ \n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1 = s - s_1.$ Since $s_1 < s,$ we have $\\varepsilon_1 > 0.$\nWe can apply the property stated in the hypothesis to our $\\varepsilon_1.$ So, there exists $a_1 \\in A$ such that\n\n$$\ns - \\varepsilon_1 < a_1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\varepsilon_1 = s - s_1,$ we get $s - (s - s_1) < a_1,$ which simplifies to\n\n$$\ns_1 < a_1.\n$$\n\nSince $s_1$ is an upper bound of $A,$ we must have $a_1 \\le s_1.$ This contradicts $s_1 < a_1.$ Thus, $s = \\sup A.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163204 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we defined the infimum of a set as the maximum of the set of lower bounds, and the supremum as the minimum of the set of upper bounds.\n\nIf a set of real numbers is bounded and not empty, then the infimum exists and is a real number, and the supremum exists and is a real number.\n\nThis follows from the so-called **axiom of completeness**, a fundamental property of the real numbers that ensures that the real number line has no \"gaps.\"\n\nThere are number sets, such as the set of rational numbers, that don't have this property. Consider for example the following subset of $\\mathbb Q{:}$\n\n$$\nA = \\{ q \\in \\mathbb Q \\, : \\, 1 < q^2 < 2 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the rational numbers greater than $\\sqrt 2$ are the upper bounds of $A.$ However, this set has no minimum *in $\\mathbb{Q}$*, so $\\sup(A)$ doesn't exist." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162184 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :question/id #uuid "533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107128" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of an exhaust pipe of a motorcycle can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe is ${140}^{∘}F,$ calculate its temperature after $3$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the exhaust pipe of a motorcycle is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-74| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-74>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-74 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-74 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-74 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =74+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe was ${140}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =74+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =74+K \\\\ K & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=74+66{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the exhaust pipe after $t=3$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(3)=74+66{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 3}≈{126}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" "answer-43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" "answer-ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" "answer-b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" "answer-339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" :answer/id #uuid "9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{112}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/id #uuid "43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{126}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" :answer/id #uuid "ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{134}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" :answer/id #uuid "b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893" :answer/id #uuid "339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{98}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4ae75f36-a304-5922-b635-26c88b77267f" :activity/id #uuid "4ae75f36-a304-5922-b635-26c88b77267f" :activity/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049131 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-5931805b-d9b9-5298-a1d9-33e2c2616d14" "step-2aa81c5c-5cf6-5623-b70e-2dcb0c85aaa1" "step-7ae547ec-c32b-530d-a28e-36ed80d0553b" "step-a73cffdd-cb0c-5cc4-9384-d7e65e74f16d" "step-36644ad5-26bc-5680-9a8d-2d22a7eb2188"] :activity/first-step "step-5931805b-d9b9-5298-a1d9-33e2c2616d14" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-5931805b-d9b9-5298-a1d9-33e2c2616d14" :step/id #uuid "5931805b-d9b9-5298-a1d9-33e2c2616d14" :step/content 17592186078879 :step/next "step-2aa81c5c-5cf6-5623-b70e-2dcb0c85aaa1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2aa81c5c-5cf6-5623-b70e-2dcb0c85aaa1" :step/id #uuid "2aa81c5c-5cf6-5623-b70e-2dcb0c85aaa1" :step/content 17592186399580 :step/next "step-7ae547ec-c32b-530d-a28e-36ed80d0553b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7ae547ec-c32b-530d-a28e-36ed80d0553b" :step/id #uuid "7ae547ec-c32b-530d-a28e-36ed80d0553b" :step/content 17592186244026 :step/next "step-a73cffdd-cb0c-5cc4-9384-d7e65e74f16d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a73cffdd-cb0c-5cc4-9384-d7e65e74f16d" :step/id #uuid "a73cffdd-cb0c-5cc4-9384-d7e65e74f16d" :step/content 17592186397884 :step/next "step-36644ad5-26bc-5680-9a8d-2d22a7eb2188" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-36644ad5-26bc-5680-9a8d-2d22a7eb2188" :step/id #uuid "36644ad5-26bc-5680-9a8d-2d22a7eb2188" :step/content "question-533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172230 :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(5).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(5)=4!=24.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168243 :answer-field/correct 17592186168248 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" :question/id #uuid "1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" :question/math-academy-id "q-107851" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(8).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(8)=7!=5\\,040.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22c5e926-ac1f-5f05-944d-e1b4e4b09520"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22c5e926-ac1f-5f05-944d-e1b4e4b09520" :answer-field/id #uuid "22c5e926-ac1f-5f05-944d-e1b4e4b09520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c305fd9c-6218-528d-9a5f-49bc8d25aa53" "answer-e98f9aaf-ae44-57d2-b3aa-a7763efa9ced" "answer-9ff974d8-7678-510e-a50c-d042422993ff" "answer-206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" "answer-98d40466-0fee-5000-9a4e-74165fc45aba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c305fd9c-6218-528d-9a5f-49bc8d25aa53" :answer/id #uuid "c305fd9c-6218-528d-9a5f-49bc8d25aa53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,080" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e98f9aaf-ae44-57d2-b3aa-a7763efa9ced" :answer/id #uuid "e98f9aaf-ae44-57d2-b3aa-a7763efa9ced" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ff974d8-7678-510e-a50c-d042422993ff" :answer/id #uuid "9ff974d8-7678-510e-a50c-d042422993ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" :answer/id #uuid "206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98d40466-0fee-5000-9a4e-74165fc45aba" :answer/id #uuid "98d40466-0fee-5000-9a4e-74165fc45aba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "720" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" "question-df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" "question-1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" :question/id #uuid "dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107857" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6aa87497-13d4-5b82-adae-cac5f16dbe31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6aa87497-13d4-5b82-adae-cac5f16dbe31" :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa87497-13d4-5b82-adae-cac5f16dbe31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" "answer-3372a2b1-d89c-5ea5-ae2d-dc597ba5d5b8" "answer-580968ec-8d67-5eee-8ed5-dc153c668ede" "answer-fa8079d4-cfe9-5db0-8ed9-f1196344fbb6" "answer-636f9395-da30-5934-b0e7-87be6c2499c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" :answer/id #uuid "93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3372a2b1-d89c-5ea5-ae2d-dc597ba5d5b8" :answer/id #uuid "3372a2b1-d89c-5ea5-ae2d-dc597ba5d5b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{\\pi }}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-580968ec-8d67-5eee-8ed5-dc153c668ede" :answer/id #uuid "580968ec-8d67-5eee-8ed5-dc153c668ede" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa8079d4-cfe9-5db0-8ed9-f1196344fbb6" :answer/id #uuid "fa8079d4-cfe9-5db0-8ed9-f1196344fbb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-636f9395-da30-5934-b0e7-87be6c2499c2" :answer/id #uuid "636f9395-da30-5934-b0e7-87be6c2499c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" :question/id #uuid "df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107859" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-58b55a76-73d7-558c-b98f-2fa3e367ce2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-58b55a76-73d7-558c-b98f-2fa3e367ce2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "58b55a76-73d7-558c-b98f-2fa3e367ce2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" "answer-961cc474-dd53-5341-baee-0b7c843c8e47" "answer-0998b024-6768-582f-945a-d5609ee9f1a1" "answer-f0a2c475-4407-520d-aa2c-30700dd13b24" "answer-8908fc27-a7eb-5475-8abd-39bad6c188d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" :answer/id #uuid "2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-961cc474-dd53-5341-baee-0b7c843c8e47" :answer/id #uuid "961cc474-dd53-5341-baee-0b7c843c8e47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0998b024-6768-582f-945a-d5609ee9f1a1" :answer/id #uuid "0998b024-6768-582f-945a-d5609ee9f1a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0a2c475-4407-520d-aa2c-30700dd13b24" :answer/id #uuid "f0a2c475-4407-520d-aa2c-30700dd13b24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8908fc27-a7eb-5475-8abd-39bad6c188d9" :answer/id #uuid "8908fc27-a7eb-5475-8abd-39bad6c188d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" :question/id #uuid "1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107861" :question/problem "$2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68ee2d15-0779-5b6d-a0a3-58d2b6c1ee1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68ee2d15-0779-5b6d-a0a3-58d2b6c1ee1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "68ee2d15-0779-5b6d-a0a3-58d2b6c1ee1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" "answer-731dc778-4a1e-5474-afe6-d3d089ddf682" "answer-7d43dd58-4629-52b1-8528-64bea432b08f" "answer-6bc959a4-5056-5435-b552-7ae39524df65" "answer-c7ee9600-62e5-57c5-a87c-047a37f4904a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" :answer/id #uuid "00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731dc778-4a1e-5474-afe6-d3d089ddf682" :answer/id #uuid "731dc778-4a1e-5474-afe6-d3d089ddf682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d43dd58-4629-52b1-8528-64bea432b08f" :answer/id #uuid "7d43dd58-4629-52b1-8528-64bea432b08f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bc959a4-5056-5435-b552-7ae39524df65" :answer/id #uuid "6bc959a4-5056-5435-b552-7ae39524df65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7ee9600-62e5-57c5-a87c-047a37f4904a" :answer/id #uuid "c7ee9600-62e5-57c5-a87c-047a37f4904a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-364c21bd-4c9d-517c-904f-c5dd46711403" :activity/id #uuid "364c21bd-4c9d-517c-904f-c5dd46711403" :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049433 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" "step-cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" "step-32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" "step-fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" "step-c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73"] :activity/first-step "step-2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" :step/id #uuid "2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" :step/content 17592186172230 :step/next "step-cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" :step/id #uuid "cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" :step/content "question-1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" :step/next "step-32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" :step/id #uuid "32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" :step/content "question-dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" :step/next "step-fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" :step/id #uuid "fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" :step/content "question-df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" :step/next "step-c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73" :step/id #uuid "c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73" :step/content "question-1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c5c07663-c2ba-5968-924a-8ce6b0b341c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5c07663-c2ba-5968-924a-8ce6b0b341c8" :question/id #uuid "c5c07663-c2ba-5968-924a-8ce6b0b341c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133791" :question/problem "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Paul's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nyards." :question/worked-solution "The width of the plot is $x+5$ yards, and its area is $3{x}^{2}+15x$ square yards. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(3{x}^{2},15x)=3x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+15x & = \\\\ (3x\\cdot x)+(3x\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x\\cdot x)+(3x\\cdot 5) & = \\\\ 3x(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $3x$ yards.\n\n![](images/13/13d17ee1a2e1157f7cf0afa191f2c4096c7c6dea6086f0b3aafd48af159aeb1a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae84797d-d8ce-5c37-8e3b-a77fc43d526c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae84797d-d8ce-5c37-8e3b-a77fc43d526c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae84797d-d8ce-5c37-8e3b-a77fc43d526c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2fe20cc7-781b-5887-8290-d7c252d770c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fe20cc7-781b-5887-8290-d7c252d770c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fe20cc7-781b-5887-8290-d7c252d770c1" :answer/id #uuid "2fe20cc7-781b-5887-8290-d7c252d770c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-77006e45-cf97-549c-aab3-71cabbd06eba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-77006e45-cf97-549c-aab3-71cabbd06eba" :question/id #uuid "77006e45-cf97-549c-aab3-71cabbd06eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-133792" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e69bcccc2f30199fd64a9479c623c107610760686c744466736b30995cc4654e.png)\n\nMary is Paul's neighbor. She has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-25$ square yards and whose width is $x+5$ yards, as depicted above. What is the length of Mary's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nMary's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nyards." :question/worked-solution "To find the length of Mary's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-25.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25={x}^{2}-{5}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=(x+5)(x-5)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x-5$ yards.\n\n![](images/1d/1d05c236e65fb4011152735b09dd7668bd6a36b119319a95ec1f3064bd13db4a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ad42b8d-3d0c-5cb4-a0f7-416d8b9e63fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ad42b8d-3d0c-5cb4-a0f7-416d8b9e63fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad42b8d-3d0c-5cb4-a0f7-416d8b9e63fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-608cddaa-ea8f-52c5-b47d-7c931969a47d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-608cddaa-ea8f-52c5-b47d-7c931969a47d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-608cddaa-ea8f-52c5-b47d-7c931969a47d" :answer/id #uuid "608cddaa-ea8f-52c5-b47d-7c931969a47d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054775 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cfee4982-2b49-58b3-8a10-a19a8ff05ec4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cfee4982-2b49-58b3-8a10-a19a8ff05ec4" :question/id #uuid "cfee4982-2b49-58b3-8a10-a19a8ff05ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-133793" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c57074e325bd05bdfd076a8f07b50b15c18099fc37bcace725201237d8034a1c.png)\n\nA utility pole is located on the edge of Paul's land, as shown above. The distance from the right edge of Paul's plot to the pole is $2x-4$ yards. Find the distance to the pole from the left edge as a function of $x.$\n\nThe required distance is \n{{field-1}}\nyards." :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that the length of Paul's plot is $3x$ yards.\n\nTo find the required distance, we need to subtract polynomials $3x$ and $2x-4:$\n\nWe put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3-2)x+4 & = \\\\ x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30186738-af2f-5bf7-abbc-5cb7b1eb80ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30186738-af2f-5bf7-abbc-5cb7b1eb80ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "30186738-af2f-5bf7-abbc-5cb7b1eb80ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-85d2709c-191b-53e9-84d8-9a092328f151" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85d2709c-191b-53e9-84d8-9a092328f151"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85d2709c-191b-53e9-84d8-9a092328f151" :answer/id #uuid "85d2709c-191b-53e9-84d8-9a092328f151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cbff1985-d682-5038-ad53-7d9761aafbae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbff1985-d682-5038-ad53-7d9761aafbae" :question/id #uuid "cbff1985-d682-5038-ad53-7d9761aafbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-133794" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ad6be914ed14fb22276982edeab75c9726c56e57e32760371db7936b8091468.png)\n\nPaul's neighbor to the south, Karol, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+2x-8$ square yards, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Karol's plot of land is $x+4$ yards, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nKarol's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nyards." :question/worked-solution "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+2x-8.$\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-8).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 8 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-4) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-8) & =-7 & \\times  \\\\ -1+8 & =7 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-4) & =-2 & \\times  \\\\ -2+4 & =2 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $2x=4x-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x-2x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x-2x-8 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(-2x-8) & = \\\\ x(x+4)-2(x+4) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ yards. \n\n![](images/b7/b7bff7b60f02cab37aef8f40d7b303627aedcc37995afc4ffbc2fe756110673a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31779eb9-4392-5feb-93cc-dfac27a0aceb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31779eb9-4392-5feb-93cc-dfac27a0aceb" :answer-field/id #uuid "31779eb9-4392-5feb-93cc-dfac27a0aceb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5aad4017-2927-590f-958a-30fed270da11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5aad4017-2927-590f-958a-30fed270da11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aad4017-2927-590f-958a-30fed270da11" :answer/id #uuid "5aad4017-2927-590f-958a-30fed270da11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078472 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-309a6ab7-981e-5132-839c-89444347093a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-309a6ab7-981e-5132-839c-89444347093a" :question/id #uuid "309a6ab7-981e-5132-839c-89444347093a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133795" :question/problem "What is the degree of the polynomial that represents the area of Karol's plot of land?\n\nDegree $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-8\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2{x}^{1}-8{x}^{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, it has the exponents $2,$ $1,$ and $0.$ The degree is the largest of these exponents, which is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3e7f01e-bff3-56f8-870d-85b97052a7da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3e7f01e-bff3-56f8-870d-85b97052a7da" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3e7f01e-bff3-56f8-870d-85b97052a7da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c669d258-f555-5b0e-8573-347e28925436" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c669d258-f555-5b0e-8573-347e28925436"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c669d258-f555-5b0e-8573-347e28925436" :answer/id #uuid "c669d258-f555-5b0e-8573-347e28925436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b044a91-329e-59b9-adc9-5aa3764882b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b044a91-329e-59b9-adc9-5aa3764882b6" :question/id #uuid "4b044a91-329e-59b9-adc9-5aa3764882b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133796" :question/problem "If $x=20\\,\\text{yd},$ what is the area of Karol's land plot?\n\nArea $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{yd}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we have to evaluate the polynomial ${x}^{2}+2x-8$ at $x=20.$\n\nSo, we substitute $x=20$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & = \\\\ (20)^{2}+2(20)-8 & = \\\\ 400+40-8 & = \\\\ 432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is $432\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a418d9f4-5901-559b-bb52-77f13f8ff2aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a418d9f4-5901-559b-bb52-77f13f8ff2aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "a418d9f4-5901-559b-bb52-77f13f8ff2aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-86c09828-8c4b-5bb3-82a8-6bc7c562bf32" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86c09828-8c4b-5bb3-82a8-6bc7c562bf32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86c09828-8c4b-5bb3-82a8-6bc7c562bf32" :answer/id #uuid "86c09828-8c4b-5bb3-82a8-6bc7c562bf32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "432" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb8e2d93-f5db-5ed4-9595-24f5393bca59"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb8e2d93-f5db-5ed4-9595-24f5393bca59" :question/id #uuid "bb8e2d93-f5db-5ed4-9595-24f5393bca59" :question/math-academy-id "q-133797" :question/problem "![](images/60/60e3ad251f3b7eed158489e287b072ee133955841c812da26f37538a539f9cd7.png)\n\nWhen $x=20\\,\\text{yd},$ the dimensions of Karol's plot of land are $24\\,\\text{yd}$ and $18\\text{yd}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nyards." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=24\\,\\text{yd}$  and $b=18\\,\\text{yd}.$\n\n![](images/35/35dff3715d9e7e06f7c15a3b80c0171820f71e1998695550a7672377d981ee76.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{24}^{2}+{18}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{576+324} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{900} \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-225cabde-5001-5e72-b484-fee55ecc4e61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-225cabde-5001-5e72-b484-fee55ecc4e61" :answer-field/id #uuid "225cabde-5001-5e72-b484-fee55ecc4e61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-50df093c-1492-56bf-94fa-09b0fea61347" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50df093c-1492-56bf-94fa-09b0fea61347"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50df093c-1492-56bf-94fa-09b0fea61347" :answer/id #uuid "50df093c-1492-56bf-94fa-09b0fea61347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5c2f07e7-1da9-5f1b-b117-db8d520999a1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c2f07e7-1da9-5f1b-b117-db8d520999a1" :question/id #uuid "5c2f07e7-1da9-5f1b-b117-db8d520999a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-133798" :question/problem "Paul purchased his land for $400$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Paul's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $400$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars \n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=644.204≈644,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $644$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7cb5d96-36e5-5198-87e5-6213f99e66e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7cb5d96-36e5-5198-87e5-6213f99e66e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cb5d96-36e5-5198-87e5-6213f99e66e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9a8cfaa5-8866-5393-910a-300c18a0c36a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a8cfaa5-8866-5393-910a-300c18a0c36a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a8cfaa5-8866-5393-910a-300c18a0c36a" :answer/id #uuid "9a8cfaa5-8866-5393-910a-300c18a0c36a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "644" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147563 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2064e26a-7656-5c06-9eb5-86690541ec19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2064e26a-7656-5c06-9eb5-86690541ec19" :question/id #uuid "2064e26a-7656-5c06-9eb5-86690541ec19" :question/math-academy-id "q-133799" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of Paul's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=400\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nPaul's land grew in value by\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first two years." :question/worked-solution "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =400\\cdot (1.1)^{(2)} \\\\  & =400\\cdot (1.21) \\\\  & =484\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =484-400 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was $84$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a4ed4ce8-2f0b-590b-a0e9-c44d682353ac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a4ed4ce8-2f0b-590b-a0e9-c44d682353ac" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ed4ce8-2f0b-590b-a0e9-c44d682353ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c96f6e21-5ed2-5d31-985c-78d70474a7a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c96f6e21-5ed2-5d31-985c-78d70474a7a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c96f6e21-5ed2-5d31-985c-78d70474a7a5" :answer/id #uuid "c96f6e21-5ed2-5d31-985c-78d70474a7a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-51a2d6a6-b60f-50cd-97ea-595fbb919894" :multistep/id #uuid "51a2d6a6-b60f-50cd-97ea-595fbb919894" :multistep/context "![](images/f8/f82a8a9f2c830772788bd5c49bf39cd7a5ada93bcab1f81fa08f247c6020d33c.png)\n\nPaul owns a rectangular plot of land, as shown above. The area of the plot is $3{x}^{2}+15x$ square yards, and its width is $x+5$ yards, where $x>10$ is a fixed constant." :multistep/steps ["step-eb340cf3-9a4b-59bd-b525-3c3b48bd8800" "step-c5ba4b12-0c9f-5215-9975-193d618dbe01" "step-ef1526ea-3dfa-5b70-b0db-89647a50d659" "step-f1af217f-8994-58dc-9df8-0632ec3ed68f" "step-117ee75c-7c36-545e-9458-12a962def7e5" "step-05319ee0-3f80-50c8-bc7c-953e1146c0d0" "step-62dfc50c-c538-518d-ba11-ac9ba2dc3489" "step-e141519e-223d-5342-a346-5ddd00872fea" "step-dc8fb9b8-33f5-5877-a384-d88c2595d85d"] :multistep/first-step "step-eb340cf3-9a4b-59bd-b525-3c3b48bd8800" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-eb340cf3-9a4b-59bd-b525-3c3b48bd8800" :step/id #uuid "eb340cf3-9a4b-59bd-b525-3c3b48bd8800" :step/content "question-c5c07663-c2ba-5968-924a-8ce6b0b341c8" :step/next "step-c5ba4b12-0c9f-5215-9975-193d618dbe01" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c5ba4b12-0c9f-5215-9975-193d618dbe01" :step/id #uuid "c5ba4b12-0c9f-5215-9975-193d618dbe01" :step/content "question-77006e45-cf97-549c-aab3-71cabbd06eba" :step/next "step-ef1526ea-3dfa-5b70-b0db-89647a50d659" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ef1526ea-3dfa-5b70-b0db-89647a50d659" :step/id #uuid "ef1526ea-3dfa-5b70-b0db-89647a50d659" :step/content "question-cfee4982-2b49-58b3-8a10-a19a8ff05ec4" :step/next "step-f1af217f-8994-58dc-9df8-0632ec3ed68f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f1af217f-8994-58dc-9df8-0632ec3ed68f" :step/id #uuid "f1af217f-8994-58dc-9df8-0632ec3ed68f" :step/content "question-cbff1985-d682-5038-ad53-7d9761aafbae" :step/next "step-117ee75c-7c36-545e-9458-12a962def7e5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-117ee75c-7c36-545e-9458-12a962def7e5" :step/id #uuid "117ee75c-7c36-545e-9458-12a962def7e5" :step/content "question-309a6ab7-981e-5132-839c-89444347093a" :step/next "step-05319ee0-3f80-50c8-bc7c-953e1146c0d0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-05319ee0-3f80-50c8-bc7c-953e1146c0d0" :step/id #uuid "05319ee0-3f80-50c8-bc7c-953e1146c0d0" :step/content "question-4b044a91-329e-59b9-adc9-5aa3764882b6" :step/next "step-62dfc50c-c538-518d-ba11-ac9ba2dc3489" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62dfc50c-c538-518d-ba11-ac9ba2dc3489" :step/id #uuid "62dfc50c-c538-518d-ba11-ac9ba2dc3489" :step/content "question-bb8e2d93-f5db-5ed4-9595-24f5393bca59" :step/next "step-e141519e-223d-5342-a346-5ddd00872fea" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e141519e-223d-5342-a346-5ddd00872fea" :step/id #uuid "e141519e-223d-5342-a346-5ddd00872fea" :step/content "question-5c2f07e7-1da9-5f1b-b117-db8d520999a1" :step/next "step-dc8fb9b8-33f5-5877-a384-d88c2595d85d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dc8fb9b8-33f5-5877-a384-d88c2595d85d" :step/id #uuid "dc8fb9b8-33f5-5877-a384-d88c2595d85d" :step/content "question-2064e26a-7656-5c06-9eb5-86690541ec19" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-76e5ce36-199d-57f8-8c5c-34ed5ab99437" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "76e5ce36-199d-57f8-8c5c-34ed5ab99437" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-51a2d6a6-b60f-50cd-97ea-595fbb919894" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186051017 17592186048928 17592186048524 17592186049074 17592186048406 17592186050396 17592186049224 17592186050886 17592186048650] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e42b526e-9c6b-52d4-8c19-e1205c38bbd7" :activity/id #uuid "e42b526e-9c6b-52d4-8c19-e1205c38bbd7" :activity/title "Factoring Polynomials Using Various Techniques" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-ae5abc43-6379-596a-a59d-29306630faff"] :activity/first-step "step-ae5abc43-6379-596a-a59d-29306630faff" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae5abc43-6379-596a-a59d-29306630faff" :step/id #uuid "ae5abc43-6379-596a-a59d-29306630faff" :step/content "assigned-problem-76e5ce36-199d-57f8-8c5c-34ed5ab99437" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064883 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063354 :answer-field/correct 17592186063356 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234376 :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{′}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-401d966c-98a8-5ffc-958c-1d19edfa6b7e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-401d966c-98a8-5ffc-958c-1d19edfa6b7e" :answer-field/id #uuid "401d966c-98a8-5ffc-958c-1d19edfa6b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f42704d7-ba6a-5ac9-b641-e73d8c7eb55c" "answer-f70d98a5-70c4-581c-95be-04705131018f" "answer-2b25b7d1-2205-5d45-9eb4-1153c39f2254" "answer-cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" "answer-9bf1ee9d-fb31-555f-8234-c02cee50db01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f42704d7-ba6a-5ac9-b641-e73d8c7eb55c" :answer/id #uuid "f42704d7-ba6a-5ac9-b641-e73d8c7eb55c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f70d98a5-70c4-581c-95be-04705131018f" :answer/id #uuid "f70d98a5-70c4-581c-95be-04705131018f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b25b7d1-2205-5d45-9eb4-1153c39f2254" :answer/id #uuid "2b25b7d1-2205-5d45-9eb4-1153c39f2254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" :answer/id #uuid "cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bf1ee9d-fb31-555f-8234-c02cee50db01" :answer/id #uuid "9bf1ee9d-fb31-555f-8234-c02cee50db01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234379 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" "answer-c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" "answer-64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" "answer-59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" "answer-43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/id #uuid "a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" :answer/id #uuid "c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" :answer/id #uuid "64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" :answer/id #uuid "59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd" :answer/id #uuid "43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85" :question/id #uuid "f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85" :question/math-academy-id "q-342572" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1735679cbf165e26ac22f1fd3076d450231ea8c20c1905ec74f008bc89ec33a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2bd876f7-79e5-597c-ac7b-34096cd74827"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bd876f7-79e5-597c-ac7b-34096cd74827" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd876f7-79e5-597c-ac7b-34096cd74827" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b9f522c8-2e41-549d-a3cd-753ef997b9f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9f522c8-2e41-549d-a3cd-753ef997b9f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9f522c8-2e41-549d-a